    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's denote the final price after all discounts as \\( P \\). The shirt is initially priced at \\$30.\n\n1. **First Discount Calculation**:\n    - The initial discount is \\(20\\%\\) off. Therefore, the sale price after the first discount is given by:\n      \\[\n      \\text{Sale Price}_{1} = \\text{Initial Price} \\times (1 - \\text{Discount})\n      \\]\n      Here, the discount is \\(20\\%\\), so:\n      \\[\n      \\text{Sale Price}_{1} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n      \\]\n\n2. **Second Discount Calculation**:\n    - Mary has a coupon offering another \\(25\\%\\) off on the sale price. So, we apply the following:\n      \\[\n      \\text{Final Price} = \\text{Sale Price}_{1} \\times (1 - \\text{ Coupon Discount})\n      \\]\n      Here, the coupon discount is \\(25\\%\\):\n      \\[\n      P = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n      \\]\n\n3. **Single Discount Requirement**:\n    - We need to find which single discount percentage \\( x \\) will result in the final price being \\$18:\n      \\[\n      P = \\text{Final Price}\n      \\]\n      Using the price scenario where it is given that the final sell price should be \\$30:\n      \\[\n      30 \\times (1 - x) = 18\n      \\]\n      \n4. **Solving for \\( x \\)**:\n      \\[\n      1 - x = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.6\n      \\]\n      \n      Thus:\n      \\[\n      x = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4 = 40\\%\n      \\]\n\nThus, the single discount that would give the same price as the two successive discounts is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{40\\%}\n\\]",
