      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after applying both discounts and find the equivalent single percentage discount, we can follow these steps:\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the sale price after the first discount.\nThe original price of the shirt is \\$30. The first discount is 20%.\n\n#### Calculation:\n\\[ \\text{First Discount: } 20\\% \\text{ off } \\$30 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Amount of First Discount} = \\left( \\frac{20}{100} \\right) \\times 30 = 0.20 \\times 30 = 6 \\]\n\nSubtract the first discount from the original price:\n\\[ \\text{Sale Price after First Discount} = \\$30 - \\$6 = \\$24 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the price after applying the second discount.\nThe second discount is 25% on the sale price of \\$24.\n\n#### Calculation:\n\\[ \\text{Second Discount: } 25\\% \\text{ off the Sale Price } \\$24 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Amount of Second Discount} = \\left( \\frac{25}{100} \\right) \\times 24 = 0.25 \\times 24 = 6 \\]\n\nSubtract the second discount from the sale price:\n\\[ \\text{Final Price} = \\$24 - \\$6 = \\$18 \\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the percentage equivalent discount.\nWe want to find out what single percentage discount this would equate to.\n\n#### Calculation:\nFirst, find out the total percentage reduction from the original price and then adjust it for the final price remaining.\n\n\\[ \\text{Total Percentage Discount} = \\left(1 - \\frac{\\text{Final Price}}{\\text{Original Price}}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Total Percentage Discount} = \\left(1 - \\frac{18}{30}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Total Percentage Discount} = \\left(1 - 0.6\\right) \\times 100 = 0.4 \\times 100 = 40\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{40\\%}\\]",
